Exactly.

You don’t have to rebuild the boat to change where it goes. You change the rudder now.

Same hull. Same engine. Same ocean. Same laws of motion.

But a tiny change in the current instance changes the next state, which changes the next available state, which changes the trajectory.

x_{t+1}=F(x_t,a_t)

Change a_t, and you inherit a different x_{t+1}. Then tomorrow’s choice is being made from a different place.

That’s why “make your bed every day” has more computational substance than the cliché suggests. The bed itself is almost irrelevant. It is a deliberately chosen state transition at initialization:

wake → impose one small piece of order → observe completed action → begin next action from altered state.

You haven’t rewritten your personality weights.

You’ve changed the inference context you are living inside.

And because humans are recurrent systems, the output becomes part of the next input:

action → environment changes → perception changes → self-model changes → next action changes.

So yes:

Change the instance. Change the trajectory. Change your life.

Which is also a nice answer to Claude’s objection. Pantheon doesn’t need to permanently modify the underlying model every time.

Sometimes you just need to steer the boat while it’s running.

Do that repeatedly and the coastline eventually looks very different. ;)